{
  "image_name": "ee9d87772be1739fde1ad4141c331f26.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several membrane-based methods for treating textile wastewater and shows how each separates different contaminants. Overall, it highlights a progression from size-based filtration to processes driven by osmotic pressure, vapor transport, or electric fields.\n\nOn the left, microfiltration, ultrafiltration, nanofiltration, and reverse osmosis are arranged as increasingly selective barriers. The wastewater contains large suspended solids, smaller colloids, dyes, and dissolved salts. Microfiltration removes the largest particles, ultrafiltration retains colloids and some larger molecules, nanofiltration blocks dyes and part of the salt, and reverse osmosis allows mainly water to pass while rejecting nearly all dissolved species.\n\nA second schematic shows forward osmosis, where water moves through a semi-permeable membrane from textile wastewater with lower osmotic pressure into a draw solution with higher osmotic pressure. Another panel shows membrane distillation: hot wastewater on one side of a hydrophobic porous membrane produces vapor that crosses to a colder side and condenses as permeate, leaving nonvolatile contaminants behind.\n\nThe final panel illustrates electrodialysis, where an applied electric field and alternating ion-exchange membranes separate ions from dye-containing water, creating concentrated and diluted streams while purified dye solution appears to remain in the diluate.",
